    </table>
    
    <h2>7. How to Implement an Equal Opportunity Policy in Your Indian Business Establishment</h2>
    <p>If you’re a founder or HR leader in India, here’s a step-by-step playbook to adopt EOE best practices — and yes, make it “ATS friendly” for your careers page.</p>
    <h3>7.1 Draft an Equal Opportunity Policy Statement</h3>
    <p>Write a clear, bold statement that includes prohibited grounds: religion, caste, race, sex, gender identity, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, pregnancy, age, and any other protected category under Indian laws. Example: <em>"[Company Name] is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We celebrate diversity and are committed to creating an inclusive environment for all employees, regardless of caste, creed, gender, disability, or sexual orientation."</em></p>
    <h3>7.2 Display the Notice in the Workplace</h3>
    <p>Even though Indian law doesn’t strictly mandate it, place a framed poster in common areas (reception, break room, notice board). Additionally, include the EOE tag in email signatures and internal HR portals. This reinforces culture.</p>
    <h3>7.3 Integrate EOE into Recruitment & ATS</h3>
    <p>Ensure every job description on your career page, LinkedIn, and Naukri contains the EOE statement. For ATS platforms, add a voluntary self-identification form (disability, gender) as an optional disclosure, protecting candidate privacy. This is also a global best practice.</p>
    <h3>7.4 Train Hiring Managers</h3>
    <p>Implicit bias training and structured interviews reduce discrimination risk. Document your training efforts — that’s what regulators (and international partners) look for.</p>
    <h3>7.5 Periodic Audits</h3>
    <p>Conduct internal pay equity and promotion audits to ensure equal opportunity isn’t just a poster, but a reality. Publish diversity metrics voluntarily — this builds trust.</p>
    
